  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how platinum-based anticancer drugs cross the cell membrane and how cells can either accumulate or expel them. It contrasts simple passive diffusion with several channel- or transporter-mediated routes. On the uptake side, platinum compounds are shown entering through the membrane directly as well as through specific proteins, including the LRRC8/VRAC channel complex and the transporters CTR1, OCT1/2/3, and OCTN1/2. These pathways all move the drug inward, increasing intracellular platinum levels.\n\nInside the cell, some of the platinum appears to reach the nucleus and bind DNA, indicating the intended cytotoxic target of these drugs. The right side of the figure highlights the opposing process: channel-mediated efflux. Here, MRP2 exports platinum species out of the cell rather than importing them. The diagram suggests that intracellular platinum can be modified before export, with GSTP1 linked to conjugation involving a solvent-accessible cysteine. In contrast, “unconjugated oxaliplatin” is depicted separately, implying a different exported form or handling pathway.\n\nOverall, the image presents platinum drug response as a balance between membrane entry, intracellular accumulation, DNA targeting, and transporter-driven efflux that may contribute to resistance.",
